, "last": "2:40 PM", "ts": 1794858021, "mins": [880], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its glossy head can flash purple, bronze or green. Inside the bill is a hard ridge that works like a saw to crack open acorns.", "vox": "sounds/voice/quiscalus-quiscula.mp3?v=1793113030", "snd": "sounds/quiscalus-quiscula.mp3?v=1793113135", "sndby": "Chris Parrish, xeno-canto XC20559, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "grackle", "cm": 31.0, "img": "cutouts/grackle.png?v=1793990895", "w": 640, "h": 513, "len": 705.0, "v": 1793990895, "beak": [0.9, 0.065, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAH4AAAD/gAAB/wAAAfwAAAf4AAAP+AAAH/wAAH/4AAD/+DAB//hgB//wYAf/88AP/+OAH//DgD//DgA//hwAfjf4Afgw8APgGOAHwA/gB4APAA8AngAOAP4AAAHmAAADgAAABwAAA="}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 9, "first": "2:57 PM", "last": "3:38 PM", "ts": 1794859049, "mins": [897, 897, 898, 899, 899, 900, 900, 936, 938], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png?v=1793031431", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "v": 1793031431, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 1, "first": "3:01 PM", "last": "3:01 PM", "ts": 1794859302, "mins": [901], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, C